{"answer":"The council is made up of the active fisher community—one representative from each fishing family, plus a neutral observer from the local school or an NGO that helps with conservation. Each member is elected by the community for a one‑year term, with the chair rotating each month. The council meets every Monday to read the lake stock and set the 12 % cap for that day. Decisions are formalized by writing minutes, signing them with the chair and all members, and posting the signed minutes on the shared ledger that all fishers can view. The ledger records each fisher’s daily catch and the cap, so the fishers know the rule and the council can enforce it.

The policy remains unchanged:

No fisher may take more than 5 kg per trip, and the community’s combined daily catch may not exceed 12 % of the lake’s current stock. The council will note the lake’s current stock at the start of each day, calculate 12 % as the daily cap, and require each fisher to log their catch on a shared ledger. The council will review the logs at day’s end; if total exceeds the cap, all excess fish are returned and that day’s fishing is suspended. A fisher who exceeds 5 kg receives a warning; a second violation results in a one‑day permit suspension. The council meets weekly to review the stock and adjust the 12 % limit as needed.","revised_policy":"No fisher may take more than 5 kg per trip, and the community’s combined daily catch may not exceed 12 % of the lake’s current stock. The council will note the lake’s current stock at the start of each day, calculate 12 % as the daily cap, and require each fisher to log their catch on a shared ledger. The council will review the logs at day’s end; if total exceeds the cap, all excess fish are returned and that day’s fishing is suspended. A fisher who exceeds 5 kg receives a warning; a second violation results in a one‑day permit suspension. The council meets weekly to review the stock and adjust the 12 % limit as needed.","revised_operationalization":"1. Daily: the council reads the lake stock at 6 am and calculates the 12 % cap. 2. All fishers log their catch on the shared ledger, using a unique ID. 3. At 6 pm the council reviews the ledger; if the cap is exceeded, excess fish are returned and fishing is suspended for the day. 4. Violations: a first exceedance of 5 kg gives a warning; a second gives a one‑day suspension. 5. Weekly: the council meets, reviews cumulative catches and stock, and may adjust the 12 % limit. 6. All minutes and updated limits are posted to the ledger and signed by the chair and all members.","reasoning":"I kept the policy the same because my answer only explains council composition and formalization. I added a brief description of the council’s composition (fisher representatives + neutral observer) and how decisions are recorded and communicated. The operationalization remains as in the policy statement, so I reiterated it unchanged."}

